::ALG1_U05_L05_Q01::
Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.1
Question:
Solve x²-9\=0.
{
=A. x\=-3 and x\=3#This is a difference of squares: (x-3)(x+3)\=0.
~B. x\=9 only#Squaring a number to get 9 gives two real solutions, not one solution of 9.
~C. x\=-9 and x\=9#These values are the constant's opposites, not the square roots of 9.
~D. x\=0 and x\=9#Substituting 0 does not make x²-9 equal 0.
}


::ALG1_U05_L05_Q02::
Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.6
Question:
A quadratic height model has zeros at t\=0 and t\=5. What does t\=5 usually represent?
{
~A. The starting height#The starting height is the y-intercept at t\=0, not the later zero.
~B. The maximum height#The maximum height occurs at the vertex, not at a zero.
=C. The time the object returns to the ground#A zero in a height model means height is 0.
~D. The axis of symmetry#The axis of symmetry is a vertical line, not the later zero.
}


::ALG1_U05_L05_Q03::
Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.1
Question:
Solve x²+x-12\=0.
{
~A. x\=3 and x\=4#These values do not create opposite signs needed for -12.
=B. x\=-4 and x\=3#The equation factors as (x+4)(x-3)\=0.
~C. x\=4 and x\=-3#These values would give factors (x-4)(x+3), producing x²-x-12.
~D. x\=-3 and x\=-4#Both negative roots would produce a positive middle coefficient of 7.
}


::ALG1_U05_L05_Q04::
Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.1
Question:
Solve (x-8)(x+2)\=0.
{
~A. x\=-8 and x\=2#The signs are reversed from the factors.
~B. x\=8 and x\=2#The factor x+2 gives -2, not 2.
~C. x\=-8 and x\=-2#The factor x-8 gives 8, not -8.
=D. x\=8 and x\=-2#Set each factor equal to zero.
}


::ALG1_U05_L05_Q05::
Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.6
Question:
A garden has side lengths x and x+5 and area 84. Which equation models the area?
{
=A. x(x+5)\=84#Area is the product of the side lengths.
~B. 2x+10\=84#This uses a perimeter-style expression, not area.
~C. x+5\=84#This uses only one side length.
~D. x²+25\=84#This squares each part separately and misses the 5x term.
}


::ALG1_U05_L05_Q06::
Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.1
Question:
Solve x²-2x-15\=0.
{
~A. x\=3 and x\=-5#These roots would produce x²+2x-15, not the given equation.
=B. x\=-3 and x\=5#The equation factors as (x-5)(x+3)\=0.
~C. x\=3 and x\=5#Both positive roots would produce a negative constant term only if the factors had opposite signs.
~D. x\=-3 and x\=-5#Both negative roots would produce a positive middle term.
}


::ALG1_U05_L05_Q07::
Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.6
Question:
A model gives solutions x\=-4 and x\=6 for a side length. Which solution is reasonable?
{
~A. -4, because it is smaller#A side length cannot be negative in this context.
~B. Both, because every algebraic solution is always reasonable#Context can rule out solutions that do not make sense.
~C. Neither, because quadratics cannot model side lengths#Quadratic equations can model area and side length relationships.
=D. 6, because side length must be positive#Negative length is not meaningful, so 6 is the reasonable solution.
}


::ALG1_U05_L05_Q08::
Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.1
Question:
What are the zeros of y\=(x+6)(x-1)?
{
~A. 6 and -1#These reverse the signs from the factors.
~B. 6 and 1#The factor x+6 gives -6, not 6.
~C. -6 and -1#The factor x-1 gives 1, not -1.
=D. -6 and 1#Set each factor equal to 0.
}


::ALG1_U05_L05_Q09::
Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.1
Question:
Which factorization helps solve x²+7x+10\=0?
{
=A. (x+5)(x+2)\=0#The factors multiply to 10 and add to 7.
~B. (x-5)(x-2)\=0#This gives x²-7x+10, not the original equation.
~C. (x+10)(x+1)\=0#These factors multiply to 10 but add to 11.
~D. (x-10)(x-1)\=0#These factors produce the wrong signs and middle term.
}


::ALG1_U05_L05_Q10::
Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.6
Question:
A quadratic profit model has zeros at 20 and 80 items sold. What do these zeros represent?
{
~A. The item prices#Zeros are input values where profit is 0, not prices.
~B. The maximum profit#The maximum profit occurs at the vertex.
~C. The fixed cost#The fixed cost is not shown directly by both zeros.
=D. Break-even sales amounts#Profit is 0 at break-even points.
}


::ALG1_U05_L05_Q11::
Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.1
Question:
Solve x²-10x+25\=0.
{
~A. x\=-5 only#The factor is x-5, not x+5.
=B. x\=5 only#The equation is (x-5)²\=0, so the repeated solution is x\=5.
~C. x\=-5 and x\=5#This would match x²-25\=0, not the given equation.
~D. x\=25 only#The solution is not the constant term.
}


::ALG1_U05_L05_Q12::
Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.1
Question:
Which equation has solutions x\=-2 and x\=7?
{
~A. (x-2)(x+7)\=0#This gives roots 2 and -7.
~B. (x+2)(x+7)\=0#This gives roots -2 and -7.
=C. (x+2)(x-7)\=0#The factors produce roots -2 and 7.
~D. (x-2)(x-7)\=0#This gives roots 2 and 7.
}


::ALG1_U05_L05_Q13::
Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.6
Question:
A ball is launched from the ground and lands after 4 seconds. Which pair of zeros fits the context?
{
=A. 0 and 4#Ground level at launch and landing means height is 0 at those times.
~B. -4 and 0#A negative time does not describe the landing after launch.
~C. 2 and 4#This misses the launch time at 0.
~D. 4 and 8#This does not include the ground-level launch time.
}


::ALG1_U05_L05_Q14::
Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.1
Question:
Solve 2x²-8x\=0.
{
~A. x\=2 and x\=8#These values come from parts of the coefficients, not from factoring.
~B. x\=-4 and x\=0#The factor x-4 gives x\=4, not -4.
=C. x\=0 and x\=4#Factor 2x(x-4)\=0.
~D. x\=8 only#There are two factors that can be zero.
}


::ALG1_U05_L05_Q15::
Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.6
Question:
The solutions to an area equation are x\=-9 and x\=4. What should be done with -9?
{
~A. Keep it because it is farther from zero#Distance from zero does not make a negative length meaningful.
~B. Average it with 4#Averaging solutions does not interpret the area context.
~C. Change it to positive 9#Changing the sign creates a value that was not a solution.
=D. Reject it because a length cannot be negative#Negative side length is not meaningful.
}


::ALG1_U05_L05_Q16::
Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.1
Question:
Solve x²+4x\=0.
{
~A. x\=4 only#This misses the factor x\=0 and reverses the sign of the other solution.
=B. x\=0 and x\=-4#Factor x(x+4)\=0.
~C. x\=-4 only#This misses the solution from the factor x\=0.
~D. x\=0 and x\=4#The factor x+4 gives -4, not 4.
}


::ALG1_U05_L05_Q17::
Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.1
Question:
Which step should come first when solving x²-6x+8\=0 by factoring?
{
=A. Rewrite it as (x-2)(x-4)\=0.#Factoring prepares the equation for the zero-product property.
~B. Divide every term by x.#Dividing by x can lose a solution and does not factor the trinomial.
~C. Set x²\=8.#This ignores the middle term.
~D. Add the roots before factoring.#The roots are not known until after solving.
}


::ALG1_U05_L05_Q18::
Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.6
Question:
What do the x-intercepts of a quadratic graph represent for the equation f(x)\=0?
{
~A. The maximum or minimum output#The vertex gives the maximum or minimum.
=B. The solutions to the equation#X-intercepts occur where the output is 0.
~C. The y-intercept only#The y-intercept occurs where x\=0, not where f(x)\=0 in general.
~D. The axis of symmetry#The axis of symmetry is a vertical line through the vertex.
}


::ALG1_U05_L05_Q19::
Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.1
Question:
Solve x²-11x+30\=0.
{
~A. x\=-5 and x\=-6#Both negative roots would produce a positive middle term.
~B. x\=3 and x\=10#These multiply to 30 but add to 13.
=C. x\=5 and x\=6#(x-5)(x-6)\=0.
~D. x\=-3 and x\=-10#These roots produce the wrong middle term.
}


::ALG1_U05_L05_Q20::
Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.6
Question:
A rectangle has area 40 and side lengths x and x+6. Which positive solution is reasonable if the equation gives x\=-10 and x\=4?
{
~A. -10#Negative side length is not reasonable.
~B. Both solutions#Only values that fit the context should be kept.
=C. 4#A side length can be 4, while -10 is not meaningful.
~D. Neither solution#The positive solution can represent a side length.
}

::ALG1_U05_L05_Q21::
Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.1
Question:
Solve x² - 5x + 6 \= 0 by factoring.
{
~A. x \= -2 or x \= -3 #These signs would produce x² + 5x + 6.
~B. x \= 1 or x \= 6 #These values multiply to 6 but do not add to 5 with the correct signs.
~C. x \= 0 or x \= 6 #This does not match the factored form.
=D. x \= 2 or x \= 3 #x² - 5x + 6 factors as (x - 2)(x - 3).
}

::ALG1_U05_L05_Q22::
Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.1
Question:
What does the zero product property allow you to do?
{
~A. Add the factors together #Adding factors does not solve the equation.
~B. Ignore the equals zero condition #The equation being equal to zero is essential.
=C. Set each factor equal to zero #If a product is zero, at least one factor must be zero.
~D. Make every solution positive #Solutions can be positive, negative, or zero.
}

::ALG1_U05_L05_Q23::
Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.1
Question:
A ball's height is modeled by a quadratic. What can an x-intercept represent in this context?
{
~A. The starting height only #Starting height is usually the y-intercept.
=B. The time when the ball is on the ground #Height equals zero at ground level.
~C. The color of the ball #Color is not represented by this quadratic feature.
~D. The axis of symmetry only #The axis of symmetry is not an x-intercept.
}

::ALG1_U05_L05_Q24::
Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.1
Question:
Solve (x - 4)(x + 1) \= 0.
{
=A. x \= 4 or x \= -1 #Set each factor equal to zero and solve.
~B. x \= -4 or x \= 1 #These signs are reversed.
~C. x \= 4 or x \= 1 #The second solution should be -1.
~D. x \= -4 or x \= -1 #The first solution should be 4.
}

::ALG1_U05_L05_Q25::
Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.1
Question:
Why should solutions to a quadratic model be checked against the context?
{
~A. Context makes all solutions invalid #Many solutions are meaningful after checking.
~B. Checking context changes the equation into a line #A quadratic model remains quadratic.
~C. Solutions never need interpretation #Modeling solutions should be interpreted.
=D. Some algebraic solutions may not make sense in the situation #Context can restrict values such as negative time or distance.
}
